{
  "data": {
    "title": "Default mode network connectivity during task execution",
    "text": "ROI definitions\nSixteen seeds defining the DMN were chosen from the current literature (Andrews-Hanna et al., 2010). The finger opposition task provided us with 14 somatomotor ROIs selected from the local peaks in the task > fixation contrast. All ROIs were defined as spheres of 6 mm in diameter.\n\nROI-to-ROI correlation and hierarchical clustering analyses\nThirty by thirty matrices of Fisher z-transformed bivariate correlation coefficients (Pearson r) were constructed using all of the 30 ROIs. These weighted correlation matrices formed the basis of the connectomic representations. A hierarchical clustering analysis was conducted to identify changes in network membership.\n\nGraph theoretical analysis\nThe correlation matrices were used to assess graph theoretical metrics of mean characteristic path length, mean local efficiency, mean clustering coefficient, mean betweenness centrality and small-worldness. The analysis was performed using the Graph-Theoretical Analysis Toolbox (GAT) based on the Brain Connectivity Toolbox (BCT). The matrices were binarized across a range of threshold densities (0.2 < T < 0.4). Metrics were compared against 20 random networks over 5000 permutations using the area under the curve (AUC) method."
  },
